# Resume Autofill Assistant - Backend

Local-first personal job-application autofill assistant. This backend covers
**Phase 1** (candidate profile schema), **Phase 2** (resume ingestion and
parsing), **Phase 3** (local profile storage), and **Phase 4** (a local-only
browser-automation prototype). There is no scraping, login automation, or
auto-submit behavior here, and automation only ever touches the local sample
forms in `sample-forms/`.

## Requirements

- Python 3.10+ (developed on 3.14)

## Setup

### 1. Create and activate a virtual environment

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
cd backend
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

macOS / Linux:

```bash
cd backend
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
```

### 2. Install dependencies

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

This installs FastAPI, Uvicorn, Pydantic, python-multipart, PyMuPDF,
python-docx, email-validator, and Playwright, plus the dev tools (pytest, httpx).

> Note: PyMuPDF and python-docx are imported lazily. If a wheel is unavailable
> for your Python version, TXT extraction and the test suite still work; PDF /
> DOCX extraction will return a clear warning until the library installs.

### 3. Install the Playwright browser (Phase 4)

The browser-automation prototype needs the Chromium binary:

```bash
python -m playwright install chromium
```

This is a one-time download. Automation features (and their tests) skip cleanly
with a clear message until this is installed.

## Run the API locally

```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

Then open the interactive docs at http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs.

### CORS for the frontend and the extension

The API enables CORS for local loopback origins only (configured in `app/main.py`):

- `http://localhost:5173` / `http://127.0.0.1:5173` - Vite dev server (Phase 3)
- `http://localhost:8000` / `http://127.0.0.1:8000` - this API, which also hosts
  the sample forms (Phase 6)
- `http://localhost:8080` / `http://127.0.0.1:8080` - any other local static server

Phase 6 additionally allows Chrome extension origins via the regex
`^chrome-extension://[a-z]{32}$`. A packed extension's origin is
`chrome-extension://<32-char id>`, and that id is not known until the unpacked
extension is loaded, so it cannot be pinned in advance. The regex matches
extension origins only - no wildcard hosts.

All of this is local-development only. Do not broaden it for any real deployment.

### Sample form hosting (Phase 6)

The repo's sample forms are mounted read-only at `/sample-forms`, because Chrome
extensions are awkward to test on `file://` URLs:

- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/basic-application.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/engineering-application.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/edge-case-labels.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/sensitive-fields.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/select-radio-checkboxes.html>

The files are unchanged, and the Playwright prototype still loads them from disk.
`SAMPLE_FORMS_DIR` overrides the directory.

### Platform fixture hosting (Phase 7)

The synthetic platform pages are mounted read-only at `/test-fixtures`, so the extension's
platform adapters can be exercised on a loopback origin:

- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/greenhouse-like.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/lever-like.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/ashby-like.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/workday-like.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/smartrecruiters-like.html>
- <http://127.0.0.1:8000/test-fixtures/platform-pages/generic-company-like.html>

These are hand-written imitations of each platform's markup patterns, not copies of any
real page. Because they are served from loopback they count as `local` for safety
purposes, while their markup still selects the matching adapter - which is how a platform
adapter gets tested without a real site being involved.

### Approving a company domain (Phase 7)

Vendor platforms are built in. Company-hosted forms are not, and there is no API for
adding one: a host must be listed in `PHASE7_APPROVED_DOMAINS` before the server starts.

```powershell
$env:PHASE7_APPROVED_DOMAINS = "careers.acme-corp.example, jobs.other-co.example"
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
```

Comma or space separated; full URLs are accepted and reduced to their host. Approval is
per host, so `acme-corp.example` does not cover `careers.acme-corp.example`. The restart
requirement is deliberate - a domain should be approved at a terminal, not from a popup
while looking at a job posting.

## Run the tests

```bash
pytest
```

## Example requests

Health check:

```bash
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
```

Extract text from a resume (returns metadata + preview):

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/resume/extract-text \
  -F "file=@/path/to/resume.pdf"
```

Parse a resume into a profile:

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/resume/parse \
  -F "file=@/path/to/resume.docx"
```

On Windows PowerShell, use `curl.exe` (not the `curl` alias) for `-F` uploads:

```powershell
curl.exe -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/resume/parse -F "file=@C:\path\to\resume.txt"
```

Parse a resume and save the profile locally (Phase 3):

```bash
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/resume/parse-and-save \
  -F "file=@/path/to/resume.txt"
```

## Profile API (Phase 3)

The reviewed candidate profile is stored locally as JSON
(`backend/app/data/profile.json` by default; override with the
`PROFILE_STORAGE_PATH` environment variable). Uploaded resume files are never
stored permanently - only the profile JSON is. `profile.json` is git-ignored;
treat it as sensitive personal data.

Endpoints:

- `GET /profile` - return the saved profile (404 if none saved).
- `PUT /profile` - save (overwrite) the profile; returns the saved profile.
- `PATCH /profile/review-status` - update `metadata.needs_review` and append a
  review note.
- `DELETE /profile` - delete the saved profile.

Example requests:

```bash
# Get the saved profile
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/profile

# Save a profile (PUT a full CandidateProfile JSON)
curl -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:8000/profile \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d @profile.json

# Mark the profile reviewed (or set needs_review true)
curl -X PATCH http://127.0.0.1:8000/profile/review-status \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"needs_review": false, "review_note": "Reviewed manually on local dashboard."}'

# Delete the saved profile
curl -X DELETE http://127.0.0.1:8000/profile
```

## Browser automation prototype (Phase 4)

The prototype fills **local sample forms only** (`sample-forms/`). It never
opens real job sites, never submits, and leaves sensitive/legal fields for
manual review. See
[`../docs/phase-4-browser-automation-prototype.md`](../docs/phase-4-browser-automation-prototype.md).

### CLI demo

Run from the `backend/` directory with a saved profile:

```bash
python -m app.scripts.run_local_autofill_demo --form basic --dry-run
python -m app.scripts.run_local_autofill_demo --form basic --headful
python -m app.scripts.run_local_autofill_demo --form engineering --headful
python -m app.scripts.run_local_autofill_demo --form engineering --headful \
    --resume-file "path/to/resume.pdf"
```

### Automation API endpoints

- `GET /automation/sample-forms` - list known local forms (`basic`,
  `engineering`, `edge-case-labels`, `sensitive-fields`,
  `select-radio-checkboxes`).
- `POST /automation/analyze` `{"form_name": "basic"}` - return an enriched
  `FillPlan` (no filling), with overrides applied.
- `POST /automation/dry-run` `{"form_name": "basic"}` - run a no-type pass, return an `AutofillResult`.
- `POST /automation/run-sample` `{"form_name": "basic", "headless": true, "dry_run": false}` - fill the local form, return an `AutofillResult`.

No automation endpoint accepts an arbitrary or external URL.

Redacted JSON reports are written under `backend/app/data/automation-reports/`
(git-ignored). Override the location with `AUTOMATION_REPORTS_DIR`.

## Field-mapping engine (Phase 5)

Phase 5 makes mapping explainable and testable. Each detected field is enriched
into a `FieldContext`, classified into a `FieldCategory` with evidence-based
confidence, checked against the sensitive-field detector, resolved to a redacted
profile value, and assigned an action (`fill` / `suggest` / `manual_review` /
`skip`). See
[`../docs/phase-5-field-mapping-engine.md`](../docs/phase-5-field-mapping-engine.md).

Services: `field_context_builder.py`, `sensitive_field_detector.py`,
`field_value_resolver.py`, `redaction.py`, `field_mapping_overrides.py`, and the
refactored `field_mapper.py`.

### Mapping override endpoints (local-only)

- `GET /mapping-overrides` (optional `?form_name=basic`) - list saved overrides.
- `POST /mapping-overrides` - create/update an override; sensitive force-fills
  are downgraded to manual review with a warning.
- `DELETE /mapping-overrides/{override_id}` - delete an override.
- `POST /mapping-overrides/apply-to-plan` `{"form_name": "basic"}` - re-analyze a
  form with overrides applied.

Overrides are stored under `backend/app/data/mapping-overrides/` (git-ignored).
Override the location with `MAPPING_OVERRIDES_DIR`. Fingerprints contain only
non-private field metadata; no profile values are stored.

## Chrome extension API (Phase 6)

Phase 6 lets a local Chrome extension get a fill plan for the page it is on. The
endpoints analyze **field metadata only**: no browser is opened, `page_url` is
never fetched, and nothing from the page is stored. See
[`../docs/phase-6-chrome-extension-integration.md`](../docs/phase-6-chrome-extension-integration.md)
and [`../extension/README.md`](../extension/README.md).

Services/models: `models/extension.py`, `services/extension_field_adapter.py`.
Mapping decisions come from the unchanged Phase 5 `build_fill_plan()`.

### Extension endpoints

- `GET /extension/health` - backend reachability plus whether a saved profile
  exists and whether it still needs review.
- `POST /extension/analyze-page` - build a fill plan from detected field metadata.
  Returns a redacted `fill_plan` for display and transient `instructions` that
  carry a real value only for safe `action="fill"` fields.
- `POST /extension/validate-page` - check whether a page URL is in scope.

Only these loopback origins are accepted; anything else gets HTTP 400:

```
http://127.0.0.1:8000    http://localhost:8000
http://127.0.0.1:5173    http://localhost:5173
http://127.0.0.1:8080    http://localhost:8080
```

### Test the extension endpoints without Chrome

```bash
# Backend status and profile review state
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/extension/health

# A real job site is rejected
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/extension/validate-page \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"page_url": "https://boards.greenhouse.io/example/jobs/1"}'

# A plan from two fields of metadata (requires a saved profile)
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/extension/analyze-page \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "page_url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/basic-application.html",
    "page_title": "Basic Application",
    "form_name": "basic-application.html",
    "fields": [
      {"field_id": "f1", "selector": "#first_name", "tag_name": "input",
       "input_type": "text", "name": "first_name", "label_text": "First name"},
      {"field_id": "f2", "selector": "#email", "tag_name": "input",
       "input_type": "email", "name": "email", "label_text": "Email"}
    ]
  }'
```

`form_name` is normalized: a sample form's file name resolves to its short name
(`basic-application.html` becomes `basic`), so a mapping override saved from the
dashboard also applies when the extension analyzes the same form.

The pytest suite covers these endpoints without Chrome, without a loaded
extension, and without network access
(`tests/test_extension_adapter.py`, `test_extension_routes.py`,
`test_extension_safety.py`).

## Real platform support (Phase 7)

Phase 7 adds a second, stricter analysis path for real application pages the user opened
themselves. `POST /extension/analyze-page` is unchanged and still local-only; the new
endpoint is separate so the local flow cannot regress. See
[`../docs/phase-7-real-application-platforms.md`](../docs/phase-7-real-application-platforms.md)
and [`../docs/platform-support-matrix.md`](../docs/platform-support-matrix.md).

Services/models: `models/platform.py`, `services/platform_allowlist.py` (which URLs are in
scope), `services/platform_policy.py` (confirmation, downgrades, final value stripping).
Mapping decisions still come from the unchanged Phase 5 `build_fill_plan()`.

Nothing here fetches a URL, resolves DNS, opens a browser, or writes to disk. Every
decision is made from the URL string plus local rules.

### Platform endpoints

- `GET /platforms/supported` - every known platform, its URL patterns, and the domains
  currently approved via `PHASE7_APPROVED_DOMAINS`.
- `POST /platforms/check-url` - the platform `detection` (advisory) and `safety` decision
  (authoritative) for a URL. Returns 200 even when the page is blocked, since this is the
  informational check the popup uses to decide what to display.
- `POST /extension/analyze-real-page` - the real-page fill plan. Requires
  `user_confirmed_real_page=true` for any non-loopback page, and returns HTTP 400 without
  it. Response adds `platform`, `safety`, `warnings`, and `unsupported_count` to the
  Phase 6 shape.

Supported hosts, matched exact-host or proper-suffix (never substring) and `https` only:

```
.greenhouse.io          .lever.co             .ashbyhq.com
.myworkdayjobs.com      .myworkdaysite.com    .smartrecruiters.com
```

Loopback origins are still accepted and still need no confirmation. Everything else is
refused with a reason, including lookalikes such as `boards.greenhouse.io.evil.example`.

The extension's `platform_id` and `platform_confidence` are advisory. The backend
re-derives the platform from the URL, so a client cannot widen its own access by claiming
to be on a supported platform.

### Test the platform endpoints without Chrome

```bash
# What is supported right now, including approved company domains
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/platforms/supported

# A supported vendor page: allowed, but confirmation required
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/platforms/check-url \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"page_url": "https://boards.greenhouse.io/example/jobs/1"}'

# A lookalike host: blocked, with a reason
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/platforms/check-url \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"page_url": "https://boards.greenhouse.io.evil.example/jobs/1"}'

# Analysis without confirmation is refused (HTTP 400)
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/extension/analyze-real-page \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "page_url": "https://boards.greenhouse.io/example/jobs/1",
    "user_confirmed_real_page": false,
    "fields": []
  }'
```

Covered by `tests/test_platform_allowlist.py`, `test_platform_policy.py`, and
`test_extension_real_platform_routes.py`, which also assert that `check-url` makes no
outbound request and that the Phase 6 endpoint's behaviour is unchanged.

### Troubleshooting

- **"Playwright could not launch Chromium"** - run `python -m playwright install chromium`.
- **"No saved profile found"** - upload and review/save a profile first (dashboard
  or `/resume` + `/profile`), then retry.
- **Fields detected but not filled** - the value may be missing in the profile
  (`missing_value`), the confidence may be below the fill threshold, or the field
  may be a textarea/checkbox/radio/file (conservative by design). Check the
  decision's `reason` and `evidence`.
- **Confidence too low** - add a local mapping override to pin the category, or
  improve the saved profile so the value resolves.
- **Sensitive field not autofilled** - this is intentional and cannot be
  overridden; sensitive/legal fields are always manual review.
- **Override not applying** - overrides match on `form_name` + `field_fingerprint`.
  Use the short form name (e.g. `basic`) and the fingerprint from the field's
  mapping (`FieldMapping.field_fingerprint`).
- **Browser opens and closes too fast** - use the CLI with `--headful --keep-open`
  to keep the window open until you press Enter.
- **"Unknown sample form"** - only the five known local forms are allowed;
  arbitrary URLs are never accepted.
- **Extension gets 400 "not a supported Phase 6 page"** - `/extension/analyze-page` is
  local-only by design. Open the form via `http://127.0.0.1:8000/sample-forms/...`, or use
  `/extension/analyze-real-page` for a supported real platform.
- **Extension gets a CORS error** - restart uvicorn after pulling the Phase 6
  changes; the `chrome-extension://` allowance lives in `app/main.py`.
- **Real page refused with "requires explicit user confirmation"** - the request had
  `user_confirmed_real_page=false`. This is enforced in the backend, so it cannot be
  bypassed by calling the API directly.
- **A company domain is still blocked after being added** - `PHASE7_APPROVED_DOMAINS` is
  read at startup, so restart uvicorn. Check `GET /platforms/supported` to see what the
  running server actually loaded, and confirm you listed the exact host (approval is per
  host, not per parent domain).
- **A supported platform is blocked over `http`** - real platforms require `https`. Only
  loopback origins may be plain `http`.
- **Everything on a real page comes back as manual review** - most likely the fields are
  custom controls (`unsupported_count` will be non-zero), which is normal on Workday. It
  can also mean no profile value resolved; check each decision's `reason`.

## Project layout

```
backend/
  app/
    main.py                       FastAPI app
    models/
      candidate_profile.py        Phase 1 Pydantic schema
      resume.py                   ExtractedResumeText, ResumeParseResult
      automation.py               Phase 4 automation models
      field_mapping.py            Phase 5 mapping models (FieldContext, etc.)
      extension.py                Phase 6 extension request/response models
      platform.py                 Phase 7 platform detection + safety models
    services/
      resume_text_extractor.py    PDF/DOCX/TXT -> text
      resume_profile_parser.py    text -> rough profile (+ AI hook stub)
      profile_storage.py          local profile JSON load/save
      form_field_detector.py      Playwright field detection
      field_context_builder.py    Phase 5 raw field -> enriched FieldContext
      sensitive_field_detector.py Phase 5 sensitive/legal detection
      field_value_resolver.py     Phase 5 category -> redacted profile value
      redaction.py                Phase 5 preview redaction helpers
      field_mapper.py             Phase 5 explainable mapping engine
      field_mapping_overrides.py  Phase 5 local override store
      extension_field_adapter.py  Phase 6 extension fields -> Phase 5 engine
      platform_allowlist.py       Phase 7 which URLs are in scope (no I/O)
      platform_policy.py          Phase 7 confirmation, downgrades, value stripping
      playwright_autofill.py      autofill runner (never submits)
      automation_report_storage.py  redacted report storage
      sample_forms.py             approved local-form registry
    api/routes/resume.py          /health, /resume/*
    api/routes/profile.py         /profile/*
    api/routes/automation.py      /automation/*
    api/routes/mapping_overrides.py  /mapping-overrides/*
    api/routes/extension.py       /extension/*
    api/routes/platform_support.py   /platforms/*
    scripts/run_local_autofill_demo.py  CLI demo
    utils/file_validation.py      extension/empty checks
    data/automation-reports/      redacted reports (git-ignored)
    data/mapping-overrides/       local overrides (git-ignored)
  tests/                          pytest suite (offline)
  pyproject.toml
```

See the design docs in [`../docs/`](../docs/):
[Phase 1](../docs/phase-1-profile-schema.md),
[Phase 2](../docs/phase-2-resume-ingestion.md),
[Phase 3](../docs/phase-3-local-dashboard.md),
[Phase 4](../docs/phase-4-browser-automation-prototype.md),
[Phase 5](../docs/phase-5-field-mapping-engine.md),
